## 7. ControllerAdvice, filters, interceptors, Security

`@ControllerAdvice`: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`
- `@ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)` → HTTP 400 with a `ProblemDetail` listing the failed DTO fields (lines 133-169). This is what turns a `@Valid` DTO failure (section 2/3) into the client-facing 400.
- `@ExceptionHandler(DataIntegrityViolationException.class)` → HTTP 404 (lines 113-124) — a DB constraint violation on insert would surface as 404 here (note: not 409).
- `@ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)` → HTTP 500 (lines 96-103); e.g. a `ConstraintViolationException` from entity-level bean validation on flush would fall here.
- `@ExceptionHandler(AccessDeniedException.class)` re-throws so Security handles it (lines 84-87).

Security config (two mutually-exclusive profiles keyed on `petclinic.security.enable`):
- Default is `petclinic.security.enable=false` (`application.properties:49`), which activates `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/security/DisableSecurityConfig.java` — `anyRequest().permitAll()`, CSRF disabled. Crucially, method security (`@EnableMethodSecurity`) is declared ONLY on `BasicAuthenticationConfig` (line 19), which is `@ConditionalOnProperty(...havingValue="true")`. So with the default config the `@PreAuthorize("hasRole(@roles.OWNER_ADMIN)")` on `addOwner` is NOT enforced — the endpoint is effectively open.
- When `petclinic.security.enable=true`: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/security/BasicAuthenticationConfig.java` — HTTP Basic, `anyRequest().authenticated()`, `@EnableMethodSecurity(prePostEnabled=true)` so `@PreAuthorize` requires role `ROLE_OWNER_ADMIN` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/security/Roles.java`). It also defines a `BCryptPasswordEncoder` bean, but that is for user authentication, not owner creation.

No custom servlet `Filter` or `HandlerInterceptor` exists in the project. `@CrossOrigin` on the controller (line 57) adds CORS response headers.

AOP aspect that fires on the persistence call: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/CallMonitoringAspect.java` — `@Around("within(@org.springframework.stereotype.Repository *)")` (line 77) wraps repository calls to count invocations and accumulate timing (JMX). Note its own comment (lines 30-31): it does not join on Spring Data JPA repositories, so under the default `spring-data-jpa` profile it does not actually instrument `save`.

